<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Bodin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1530–<a href="/years/1596">1596</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Bodin was a French jurist and political philosopher, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> and professor of law at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, who wrote amid the religious conflict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>. His best-known work, The Six Books of the Republic (1576), defined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> as the absolute and perpetual power of a Republic and favoured a strong national monarchy as an antidote to factional strife. At the Estates-General at Blois he opposed a new war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>. He also wrote On the Demonomania of the Sorcerers (1580) and the Colloquium heptaplomeres (1588), a dialogue of seven men of different faiths, and died in Laon during a plague epidemic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-bodin.jpg" alt="Jean Bodin" width="330" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Stuerhelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Bodin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jean Bodin</b>, and later the bishop Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet, tutor to Louis XIV’s heir, held that the king remained bound by divine and natural law and by the fundamental laws of the kingdom, such as the rules of succession, which no king could alter.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Defenders of hereditary succession, including <b>Jean Bodin</b> in his Six Books of the Commonwealth (1576), argued that elections bred faction and civil war, while Polish nobles valued election because each candidate had to promise to respect their privileges, as Wenceslaus II of Bohemia did in the first such…</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Giovanni Botero and Antonio Serra wrote in Italy, <b>Jean Bodin</b> in France.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Cicero defended monarchy, or a mixed monarchy and oligarchy, in his theoretical works, and <b>Jean Bodin</b> identified monarchy with republic.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
